 ## Overview
-Segment Tracing is a ray marching algorithm that computes local Lipschitz bounds on candidate segments to determine adaptive step sizes, reducing the number of field function queries compared to global Lipschitz bound methods like Sphere Tracing [S1]. It is specifically designed for hierarchical skeletal implicit surfaces, leveraging the composition of distance functions and kernel functions to compute directional Lipschitz bounds [S2]. Unlike Sphere Tracing, which uses a global bound that limits step size efficiency, Segment Tracing allows larger, safer steps by adapting to the local gradient and field values [S1].
+Segment Tracing is a ray marching algorithm that computes local Lipschitz bounds on candidate segments to determine adaptive step sizes, reducing the number of field function queries compared to global Lipschitz bound methods like Sphere Tracing [S1]. It works for a particular class of hierarchical skeletal implicit surfaces, leveraging the composition of distance functions and kernel functions to compute directional Lipschitz bounds [S2, S3]. Unlike Sphere Tracing, which uses a global bound that limits step size efficiency, Segment Tracing allows larger, safer steps by adapting to the local gradient and field values [S1].
 
